WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.840
I wish I could afford the rent.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.560
There is a big problem
with air pollution.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:09.920
The city is growing

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.400
without any kind of order
or any kind of planning.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.160
Half the world's population
live in them,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.640
and by the end of the century
it will be more like 90%.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.320
Over the last sort of 20 years,
we've been talking about

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.320
urbanisation is
the way forward,

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.200
but we've seen during Covid,
people's dynamics

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.760
and priorities
are really changing

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.840
so this is a real moment in
time where we have to rethink

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.800
and almost get behind
a new vision of the city.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.200
ARCHIVE NARRATOR: Ever watch
the morning traffic

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.840
when people are going to work?

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.240
Mostly it's one car,
one person,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.000
a tonne or two of metal

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.600
that takes up 130 square feet
of space, more or less.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.280
The main issue of cities
is that they are suffering

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.360
from too many cars.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.720
Excessive traffic, the noise...

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.640
It's very difficult
to stay calm

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.600
in such a stressful
environment

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.640
as a big city.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.240
So often we design the city

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.520
with a view to optimising
the city

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.080
for the paid daytime
labour market.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.120
So we think about, how many
people can we get into the city

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.520
in the morning and out of
the city in the evening?

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.880
Rather than travelling in
from the suburbs to the centre

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.160
to work, shop and socialise,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.720
the 15-minute city
takes a different approach.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.120
The 15-minute city
is a really simple concept,

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.080
in many ways.
It's really just saying that

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.160
every urban citizen should be
able to meet their basic needs

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.600
within a 15-minute walk
or cycle ride from their house.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.440
The concept of the 15-minute
city was proposed by

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.360
French-Colombian scientist
Carlos Moreno,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.240
and aspects of it
are starting to be adopted

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.880
in cities like Paris,
Barcelona and Bogotá.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.680
It's been described as a
return to a local way of life.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.640
New technologies creates
a lot of opportunities

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:01.760
to work from home,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.040
to do more things
in the neighbourhood,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.320
to get rid of this separation
of work and living.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.360
That also would reduce

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.640
the pollution
that goes with traffic.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.320
The 15-minute city revolves
around three basic principles.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.520
Before the Covid pandemic,

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.080
the average UK worker

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.000
spent 400 days of their lives
commuting.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.840
That's enough time to read

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.840
the entire Harry Potter series
159 times.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.440
When we foreground
infrastructure

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.920
or we foreground cars,
we strip out

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.480
the thing that makes the city
the city,

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.320
which is the interaction of
people and creation of culture.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.760
When we get rid of all those
parked cars in the roads

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.880
and all the traffic,
we create lots of space

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.160
for greening the city
with trees

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.640
and this also creates
lots of opportunities

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.440
to meet your friends
in the street.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.400
My favourite thing
about living in the city -

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.640
the quality of public space,
which encourage you of

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.840
spending a lot of time outside
and meeting with other people.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.960
Implementing
the 15-minute city

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.400
would mean a significant
redesign of our infrastructure.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.920
Is that feasible?

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.840
In many cities across the
world,

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.320
we've had kind of 50 years
of planning policy

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.560
that is about zoning,

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.360
which is about separating
different types of activity

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.440
in the city where you would go
for shopping

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.440
or for work or for home life.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:46.760
And so to be able to make that
kind of pivot into a 15-minute

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.080
or a localised neighbourhood is
a huge stretch for some places.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.000
The concept of the 15-minute
city also has its critics.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.080
Some fear it could ghettoise
poorer people

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.440
whose neighbourhoods don't have
the jobs and amenities

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.560
found in more affluent
communities.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.000
A lot of places do not have
enough infrastructure,

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.960
especially places
where low-income groups live.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.960
And it's a major, major wealth
and economic gap there.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.400
It's clear that there are
different levels

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.200
of liberty and freedom
to access public space,

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.360
depending on all sorts of
categories

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.280
of identity, including gender,

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.640
but also race,
also levels of poverty.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.120
We have an imbalance about who
gets to shape public spaces,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.080
who gets to make those
decisions.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.720
So if we can democratise that
and if we can open up

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.280
and have more participation

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.640
about what kind of spaces
do we want to live in,

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.200
then we have more likelihood

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.760
that our spaces
are going to suit more people.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.160
TON VONEHOVEN:
Currently we have

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.880
a very strong decline
of biodiversity,

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.800
partially due to
agriculture practices,

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.080
but also because of
urbanisation.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.080
If we green the city, we create
more opportunity for rainwater

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.200
infiltrating in the soil.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.560
That will also enhance
a healthy ecosystem

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.080
and I think if we look into the
future, the next generations,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.280
what do we want to have
those people experience -

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.040
a rich biodiversity
or a poor biodiversity?

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.440
This is the choice
that we have to make,

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.680
and with the 15-minute city,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.480
this is a way to solve
this crisis.

